 releasing water; hydrolysis splits them again."],["B1.1","Carbohydrates and lipids","Phospholipids form bilayers because they are [amphipathic]: a hydrophilic phosphate head and hydrophobic tails."],["B1.2","Proteins","A protein's [tertiary] structure is its 3D folding, held by ionic bonds, hydrogen bonds, disulfide bridges and hydrophobic interactions."],["B1.2","Proteins","Heat and extreme pH can [denature] a protein by breaking the bonds that hold its shape."],["B2.1","Membranes and membrane transport","[Facilitated diffusion] moves molecules down a gradient through channel or carrier proteins, with no ATP."],["B2.1","Membranes and membrane transport","The sodium-potassium pump moves 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in per [ATP] used: active transport."],["B2.2","Organelles and compartmentalization","The nuclear membrane separates [transcription] from translation in eukaryotes."],["B2.3","Cell specialization","Surface area to volume ratio [decreases] as a cell grows, which limits cell size."],["B3.1","Gas exchange","Alveoli have a large surface area, one-cell-thick walls and a dense [capillary] network for fast diffusion."],["B3.2","Transport","Arteries have thick walls with elastic and muscle tissue to withstand and maintain high [pressure]."],["B3.3","Muscle and motility (HL)","In the sliding filament model, [myosin] heads bind actin and use ATP to pull the filaments past each other."],["B4.1","Adaptation to environment","A [habitat] is the place where an organism lives; adaptations are traits that suit it to that habitat."],["B4.2","Ecological niches","The competitive exclusion principle: two species cannot share the same [niche] for long in the same place."],["C1.1","Enzymes and metabolism","A [competitive] inhibitor binds the active site; a non-competitive one binds elsewhere and changes the active site's shape."],["C1.1","Enzymes and metabolism","Enzymes lower the [activation energy] of a reaction without being used up."],["C1.2","Cell respiration","Anaerobic respiration in humans makes [lactate]; in yeast it makes ethanol and CO₂."],["C1.2","Cell respiration","Most ATP in aerobic respiration is made by [chemiosmosis]: protons flow through ATP synthase."],["C1.3","Photosynthesis","In the light-dependent reactions, [photolysis] splits water, releasing oxygen as a by-product."],["C1.3","Photosynthesis","In the Calvin cycle, the enzyme [RuBisCo] fixes CO₂ onto RuBP."],["C2.1","Chemical signalling (HL)","Steroid hormones are lipid-soluble, so they pass the membrane and bind [intracellular] receptors."],["C2.2","Neural signalling","A nerve impulse is an [action potential]: depolarisation as Na⁺ flows in, then repolarisation as K⁺ flows out."],["C2.2","Neural signalling","Myelination speeds up impulses by [saltatory] conduction, jumping between nodes of Ranvier."],["C3.1","Integration of body systems","The [cerebellum] coordinates skeletal muscle movement and balance."],["C3.2","Defence against disease","[Antibodies] are made by plasma cells, which come from activated B lymphocytes."],["C3.2","Defence against disease","Vaccines make [memory cells] so a second infection gets a faster, bigger response."],["C4.1","Populations and communities","Population growth often follows a [sigmoid] curve: exponential growth, then a plateau at carrying capacity."],["C4.2","Transfers of energy and matter","Only about [10]% of energy passes from one trophic level to the next; the rest is lost, mostly as heat."],["D1.1","DNA replication","DNA replication is [semi-conservative]: each new double helix keeps one original strand."],["D1.1","DNA replication","DNA polymerase III can only add nucleotides in the [5' to 3'] direction."],["D1.2","Protein synthesis","During translation, tRNA anticodons pair with mRNA [codons] at the ribosome."],["D1.3","Mutations and gene editing","A base substitution can change one amino acid, as in [sickle cell] anaemia (GAG → GTG)."],["D2.1","Cell and nuclear division","Crossing over in prophase I and [random orientation] in metaphase I both make meiosis produce variation."],["D2.2","Gene expression (HL)","[Methylation] of DNA usually switches genes off: an epigenetic change."],["D2.3","Water potential","Water moves from higher to [lower] water potential."],["D3.1","Reproduction","[Meiosis] halves the chromosome number so fertilisation can restore it."],["D3.2","Inheritance","A [test cross] with a homozygous recessive individual reveals an unknown genotype."],["D3.3","Homeostasis","Blood glucose is controlled by [negative feedback]: insulin lowers it, glucagon raises it."],["D4.1","Natural selection","Natural selection needs [variation] that is heritable, overproduction of offspring and competition."],["D4.2","Stability and change","A [keystone] species has a much bigger effect on its ecosystem than its numbers suggest."],["D4.3","Climate change","Melting ice lowers [albedo], so more heat is absorbed: a positive feedback loop."]],"ENGLISH_TIPS":[["Paper 1","Open with a thesis that answers the guiding question AND names the text's purpose and audience. One sentence, no retelling."],["Paper 1","Use the chain technique → effect → purpose: 'The second-person address (\"you\") draws the reader in, which makes the call to donate feel personal.'"],["Paper 1","Spend the first 15 minutes annotating: text type, audience, purpose, tone, structure, then 3-4 techniques with quotes."],["Paper 1","Analyse the visuals too: layout, colour, font, image framing, gaze and vectors count as techniques in non-literary texts."],["Paper 1","Embed short quotes (2-6 words) in your own sentence instead of dropping in long ones."],["Paper 1","Structure by idea, not by line order: each paragraph proves one part of your thesis."],["Paper 1","HL: you write two essays in 2h15. Plan both briefly before writing so the second one isn't rushed."],["Paper 2","Compare in EVERY paragraph: 'Whereas Author A..., Author B...' Don't write one half on each work."],["Paper 2","Pick the question whose key word fits BOTH works well, then define that key word in your introduction."],["Paper 2","Prepare 4-5 short quotes per work by heart, each tagged with 2-3 themes so they fit many questions."],["Paper 2","Talk about choices: 'Morrison chooses a fragmented structure to...' shows awareness of the writer at work."],["Paper 2","Context only when it explains meaning: a sentence of context, then back to the text."],["Individual oral","Name your global issue as a specific claim, not a topic: not 'gender', but 'how media pressures women to conform to beauty ideals'."],["Individual oral","Split roughly evenly: about 5 minutes on the literary extract, 5 on the non-literary one, linking both to the global issue."],["Individual oral","Bring the extracts (max 40 lines each) and a plan of up to 10 bullet points, no full sentences."],["HL essay","1200-1500 words on one work or a body of work; the line of inquiry should be focused and arguable."],["HL essay","Cite secondary sources if you use them, but your own analysis has to be the backbone."],["Every answer","Swap 'shows' for precise verbs: conveys, evokes, undermines, foregrounds, juxtaposes, satirises."],["Every answer","Name the technique precisely: 'tricolon', 'anaphora', 'direct address', 'juxtaposition', 'modality', 'nominalisation'."],["Every answer","Avoid 'I think' or 'in my opinion'; argue with evidence: 'This suggests...', 'Arguably...'."],["Every answer","End paragraphs by linking back to the question, not with a summary of the quote."],["Every answer","Conclusion: answer the question in one fresh sentence, then say what this reveals about the writer's purpose or the text's wider meaning."],["Criterion B","Analysis and evaluation: say how effective a choice is, not just what it is ('The statistic is persuasive but cold...')."],["Criterion C","Focus and organisation: topic sentences that make claims, plus clear transitions between ideas."],["Criterion D","Language: vary sentence length, keep a formal register, and use literary/linguistic terms accurately."]],"GRADE_KINDS":["MYA","Mock","Paper 1","Paper 2","Paper 3","Unit test","Quiz","IA draft","Oral","Essay","Predicted","Other"],"VISION_PULL":"qwen2.5vl:3b","STUDY_HINTS":["google docs","google slides","docs.google","- word","microsoft word","powerpoint","onenote","notion","obsidian","goodnotes","notability",".pdf",".docx",".pptx","pdf","notes","worksheet","past paper","pastpaper","paper 1","paper 2","paper 3","markscheme","mark scheme","kognity","managebac","quizlet","anki","revision","revise","homework","assignment","internal assessment","textbook","chapter","flashcards","save my exams","savemyexams","khan academy","ib ","ibdp","exam","study","syllabus","toc ","ia draft","essay","lab report","practice questions","question bank","classroom"],"STUDY_APPS":["winword.exe","powerpnt.exe","onenote.exe","onenoteim.exe","acrord32.exe","acrobat.exe","sumatrapdf.exe","foxitpdfreader.exe","notion.exe","obsidian.exe","anki.exe","goodnotes.exe","excel.exe"],"SUBJ_STOP":["a","an","and","are","as","at","be","by","chrome","com","copy","docs","document","draft","edge","edit","file","final","firefox","for","format","from","google","help","home","http","https","in","index","insert","is","microsoft","my","new","note","notes","of","on","or","org","page","pdf","powerpoint","results","search","sheets","slides","tab","that","the","this","to","tools","untitled","version","view","was","were","with","word","www","your","youtube"],"FR_IRREG":{"être":[["suis","es","est","sommes","êtes","sont"],"été","ser","ét","avoir"],"avoir":[["ai","as","a","avons","avez","ont"],"eu","aur","av","avoir"],"aller":[["vais","vas","va","allons","allez","vont"],"allé","ir","all","être"],"faire":[["fais","fais","fait","faisons","faites","font"],"fait","fer","fais","avoir"],"pouvoir":[["peux","peux","peut","pouvons","pouvez","peuvent"],"pu","pourr","pouv","avoir"],"vouloir":[["veux","veux","veut","voulons","voulez","veulent"],"voulu","voudr","voul","avoir"],"devoir":[["dois","dois","doit","devons","devez","doi